EXT. SALT-LICK CORRAL - DAWN

A split-rail corral at the edge of the badlands. A single bare cottonwood. Pink light climbing a ridge. Frost on the rails. In the middle, a rangy bay horse tosses its head, tethered to a post.

Three people.

OLD BASIL TREADWELL (70s, rancher, leather vest, white whiskers) stands at the rail, rolling a cigarette. His grown daughter JUNIPER TREADWELL (30s, braid under a battered hat, rifle on her back) crouches at the horse's hoof, pretending to inspect it. And a hired hand, SEVERIN KOZLOWSKI (30s, quiet, one blue eye and one brown) leans on the post, coiling a lariat.

Nobody speaks. Basil licks the paper. Juniper runs a thumb over the horse's fetlock.

BASIL
Got a stone in there?

JUNIPER
Not yet.

BASIL
Then why're you looking?

JUNIPER
Habit.

Severin lets the rope fall. The horse stamps. Frost smokes off the ground.

BASIL
Your mother loved this animal.

JUNIPER
I remember.

BASIL
She'd have fed it sugar every morning.

JUNIPER
I remember that too.

Severin shifts his weight. Basil does not look at him. Juniper does.

BASIL
Severin, fetch my saddle.

SEVERIN
It's in the barn, sir.

BASIL
I know where it is.

Severin doesn't move. The horse tugs at the tether.

JUNIPER
Pa.

BASIL
Don't Pa me.

JUNIPER
He doesn't have to go.

BASIL
He's a hired hand.

JUNIPER
He's staying.

Basil finally looks at Severin. A long moment. The old man's eyes are wet but it could be the cold.

BASIL
Fine.

He finishes rolling the cigarette. Strikes a match on the rail. The flame trembles in the dawn light. He lights it. Smoke curls up past the cottonwood.

JUNIPER
You never answered me.

BASIL
About what?

JUNIPER
About the letter.

BASIL
What letter?

JUNIPER
The one you wrote and didn't send.

Basil takes a long drag. Severin takes a step toward the horse. Juniper rises.

BASIL
(very quietly)
Burn it.

JUNIPER
I read it.

BASIL
Then you know.

JUNIPER
I know you sold the north pasture.

Silence. The horse breathes steam. Severin takes the lariat and slings it over his shoulder. Basil hurls the cigarette to the dirt and grinds it under his heel.

BASIL
To him.

He doesn't point. He doesn't need to. Severin looks at the ground. Juniper looks at Severin. Then at her father. A thin wind rattles the frost off the cottonwood leaves.

JUNIPER
You sold it to the hired man.

BASIL
It was fair.

JUNIPER
It was my mother's land.

BASIL
It was my land.

JUNIPER
She gave it to you.

BASIL
And I gave it to him. Because he asked.

Severin lifts his head. Juniper lunges. He sidesteps; she slams into the post, hand on his shirt, knuckles white. The horse shies, ropes snapping taut. Basil steps between them, one arm up, the old man suddenly very strong.

BASIL (CONT'D)
Enough!

They freeze: Juniper with her fist in Severin's shirt, Severin with both hands raised, Basil with his arm between. The horse screams and settles. Nobody breathes.

JUNIPER
(low, to Severin)
You didn't tell me.

SEVERIN
He asked me not to.

JUNIPER
(to her father)
Why?

BASIL
Because you'd have fought. Like you're fighting now.

She lets go of the shirt slowly, one finger at a time. Steps back. Wipes her mouth. Looks at the pink light cresting the ridge.

JUNIPER
(muttering)
Four generations.

BASIL
Five, if you stay.

She doesn't answer. She unslings the rifle, props it against the post, and goes to the horse. She puts her cheek against its neck. Basil and Severin stand there, apart, while the sun lifts over the ridge and floods the frozen yard.
